Why This Is Asked

Tests practical ability to design a scalable, tenant-aware data intake: dynamic schemas, low-latency validation, and reliable processing.

Key Concepts

- Event-driven ingestion with per-tenant schema discovery
- Caching and TTL for schema lookups
- Fast validation library (e.g., Ajv)
- Idempotent writes and deduplication
- Dead-letter queue for bad data
- Schema evolution testing and compatibility checks

Code Example

```javascript
// Ingestion function (Node.js)
const ajv = new Ajv();
let schemaCache = new Map();
async function getSchemaForTenant(tenantId){
  if(schemaCache.has(tenantId)) return schemaCache.get(tenantId);
  const cfg = await AppConfigClient.getConfigurationSetting({key: `schema:${tenantId}`});
  const schema = JSON.parse(cfg.value);
  schemaCache.set(tenantId, schema);
  setTimeout(() => schemaCache.delete(tenantId), 60_000);
  return schema;
}
async function validateAndRoute(msg){
  const tenantId = msg.tenantId;
  const schema = await getSchemaForTenant(tenantId);
  const validate = ajv.compile(schema);
  const valid = validate(msg.payload);
  if(!valid){ await DLQ.push({tenantId, msg, errors: validate.errors}); return; }
  await CosmosDB.upsert('events', tenantId, msg.payload, {dedupId: msg.id});
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you test schema evolution across tenants and ensure backward compatibility?
- How would you handle schema removal or deprecation without breaking in-flight events?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design lightweight auditing with low latency using Append Blobs and JWT parsing; demonstrates immutability, data-audit separation, and basic error handling.

## Key Concepts
- Append Blob usage for immutable, append-only logs
- JWT payload extraction from Authorization header
- Per-event audit entries with timestamp and eventId
- Resilient writes with basic retry/backoff

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Azure Function snippet (TypeScript)
import { ContainerClient, AppendBlobClient } from "@azure/storage-blob";
const containerClient = new ContainerClient("<storage-url>", new DefaultAzureCredential());
export async function run(context, req){
  const auth = req.headers["authorization"] ?? "";
  const token = auth.split(" ")[1] ?? "";
  const payload = JSON.parse(Buffer.from(token.split(".")[1], 'base64').toString());
  const userId = payload?.sub ?? "anonymous";
  const eventId = req.body?.eventId ?? crypto.randomUUID();
  const line = JSON.stringify({ ts: new Date().toISOString(), eventId, userId, status: 'received' }) + "\n";
  const audit = containerClient.getAppendBlobClient("audit.log");
  await audit.appendBlock(line, line.length);
  context.res = { status: 200, body: { eventId } };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to handle audit log rotation or retention?
- How would you enforce per-tenant isolation for audits?

